<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Idle-WAKEUP overlay for the Cardo reference stack.

  This is a different problem from its neighbour 10-cardo-quiet.xml, which is about how much
  ClickHouse WRITES about itself. This file is about how often it WAKES UP to check whether there is
  anything to do. Silencing the system logs removed the disk churn and left roughly 5% of one core
  burning on a server with no traffic at all.

  MEASURED, on this stack, idle, by sampling utime+stime per thread out of /proc over 30 seconds and
  grouping by thread name:

      ThreadPool         133 ticks   4.43% of one core   (16 threads)
      MemoryWorker        10 ticks   0.33% of one core
      clickhouse-serv      3 ticks   0.10% of one core
      ExternalLoader       2 ticks   0.07% of one core
      ConfigReloader       2 ticks   0.07% of one core
      AsyncMetrics         1 tick    0.03% of one core

  Note what is NOT there: no merges, no log flushing, no query work. 10-cardo-quiet.xml had already
  dealt with those. What is left is pure polling, and the dominant term is a handful of periodic
  tasks that fire per table whether or not that table has changed. The defaults below were read out
  of system.merge_tree_settings and system.server_settings on the running server rather than guessed:

    merge_tree_clear_old_parts_interval_seconds = 1

  Once a second, per MergeTree table, forever. Cardo has seven of them. That single default is the
  largest contributor here, and on a store that is written once a day it is checking for stale parts
  about 86,000 times between writes.

  WHY THE DEFAULTS ARE LIKE THIS, so nobody reads them as a bug: they are correct for the machine
  ClickHouse is designed for, a dedicated multi-node cluster under continuous ingest where a part
  becomes collectable within seconds of arriving and 512 scheduling threads are all doing real work.
  Cardo's reference box is the opposite: one node, seven tables, one write per day. Nothing here is a
  fix for ClickHouse. It is a statement that this deployment is idle nearly all of the time.

  TRADE-OFF: background housekeeping now happens on the order of minutes rather than seconds, so
  merged-away source parts linger a few minutes longer on disk and a merge may be selected up to
  five minutes after the write that made it worthwhile. For a store written once a day by a poller,
  neither is observable. Under continuous ingest both would matter, which is why this file is scoped
  to the evaluation stack and not advice for a production cluster.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!-- Per-table periodic housekeeping. These fire on a timer per MergeTree table regardless of
         whether the table was touched, so their cost scales with table count, not write volume. -->
    <merge_tree>
        <merge_tree_clear_old_parts_interval_seconds>300</merge_tree_clear_old_parts_interval_seconds>
        <merge_tree_clear_old_temporary_directories_interval_seconds>300</merge_tree_clear_old_temporary_directories_interval_seconds>

        <!-- How long a table waits before re-asking "is a merge worth doing". The idle backoff
             multiplies this by merge_selecting_sleep_slowdown_factor up to the max, so raising the
             ceiling is what actually lets a quiet table go properly quiet. -->
        <merge_selecting_sleep_ms>30000</merge_selecting_sleep_ms>
        <max_merge_selecting_sleep_ms>300000</max_merge_selecting_sleep_ms>

        <cleanup_delay_period>300</cleanup_delay_period>
        <max_cleanup_delay_period>900</max_cleanup_delay_period>
    </merge_tree>

    <!-- Thread pools. A parked thread is nearly free, but the scheduler still walks these pools, and
         512 is sized for a replicated cluster coordinating fetches, mutations and a replication
         queue. None of that exists on a single node: see risks.md #11, which is the note saying this
         stack has only ever run on one. -->
    <background_schedule_pool_size>16</background_schedule_pool_size>

    <!-- background_pool_size and background_common_pool_size are deliberately LEFT AT DEFAULTS.
         Lowering background_pool_size to 4 makes ClickHouse refuse to start:

           Code: 36. The value of 'number_of_free_entries_in_pool_to_execute_mutation' setting (20)
           is greater than the value of 'background_pool_size' *
           'background_merges_mutations_concurrency_ratio' (8). This indicates incorrect
           configuration because mutations cannot work with these settings. (BAD_ARGUMENTS)

         The server sanity-checks the ratio, so the change is not one setting but four. It is also
         not where the cost is: unlike the schedule pool, these threads are created on demand for
         actual merges, and the measurement above found no merge activity to speak of. Paying for
         three coupled threshold changes to shrink a pool that is empty at idle is how a tuning file
         turns into a liability. -->

    <!-- Pools for features this deployment does not use at all: no replicas to fetch from, no
         tiered storage to move between, no Distributed tables, no Kafka or RabbitMQ engines, no
         Buffer tables. Sized to the minimum rather than removed, so the features still work if a
         future deployment turns one on. -->
    <background_fetches_pool_size>2</background_fetches_pool_size>
    <background_move_pool_size>2</background_move_pool_size>
    <background_distributed_schedule_pool_size>2</background_distributed_schedule_pool_size>
    <background_message_broker_schedule_pool_size>2</background_message_broker_schedule_pool_size>
    <background_buffer_flush_schedule_pool_size>2</background_buffer_flush_schedule_pool_size>

    <!-- The heavy async metrics walk the data directory and stat every part. 10-cardo-quiet.xml
         already stopped the RESULT being recorded; this stops it being computed so often. -->
    <asynchronous_heavy_metrics_update_period_s>600</asynchronous_heavy_metrics_update_period_s>

    <!-- Auto-sized from host RAM, which on a developer laptop means ClickHouse reserves gigabytes of
         address space to cache a dataset measured in tens of kilobytes. The uncompressed cache is
         not used for MergeTree reads unless a query asks for it, so it is switched off outright. -->
    <mark_cache_size>536870912</mark_cache_size>
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>

    <!-- Nothing here resolves a hostname after startup: the poller connects by address and there are
         no remote replicas. -->
    <dns_cache_update_period>600</dns_cache_update_period>

    <!-- THE ACTUAL ANSWER, and the reason the rest of this file moved the number so little.
         Everything above was inferred from timer defaults and was worth roughly half a percent. The
         dominant cost was found instead by taking stack traces of the busy threads:

           SELECT thread_id, thread_name,
                  arrayStringConcat(arrayMap(x -> demangle(addressToSymbol(x)), trace), ' <- ')
           FROM system.stack_trace WHERE thread_id IN (...)

         Every busy thread, without exception, was parked in pthread_cond_timedwait inside
         DB::AsynchronousInsertQueue. Sixteen of them, the default async_insert_threads, each waking
         on async_insert_busy_timeout_ms, which is 200ms. Eighty wakeups a second on a server with
         no traffic, for a feature that coalesces small concurrent inserts from many clients.

         Cardo writes one batch per source per day. There is nothing to coalesce, and the poller now
         sends async_insert=0 on its own inserts regardless of what any server is configured to do
         (see Insert in internal/store/clickhouse/client.go, where the reason is correctness rather
         than cost). Zero here disables the queue outright, which is what removes the threads.

         Worth noting for anyone reading this on a different version: async_insert defaults to ON in
         26.x. It did not always, and the flip is invisible until you go looking for it. -->
    <async_insert_threads>0</async_insert_threads>

    <!-- With the queue gone this log has no writer left, and an enabled system log still owns a
         flush timer that fires on its own schedule whether or not it has rows. -->
    <asynchronous_insert_log remove="1"/>

    <!-- The two logs 10-cardo-quiet.xml deliberately keeps. Flushing them once a minute instead of
         every 7.5 seconds costs nothing: they are read by a human after something went wrong, never
         by a dashboard. -->
    <query_log>
        <flush_interval_milliseconds>60000</flush_interval_milliseconds>
    </query_log>
    <error_log>
        <flush_interval_milliseconds>60000</flush_interval_milliseconds>
    </error_log>
    <!-- Last term. With the insert queue gone, the busiest remaining thread was MemoryWorker, which
         reconciles the memory tracker against the cgroup and purges unused jemalloc pages. Left at
         0 it picks its own period from the memory source, and on this box that was frequent enough
         to be two thirds of everything left. Measured, same 30s sampler:

             async_insert_threads=0 alone            0.40% of one core
             plus memory_worker_period_ms=2000       0.17% of one core

         TRADE-OFF: ClickHouse notices memory pressure up to two seconds later and returns freed
         pages to the OS less eagerly. On a box holding tens of kilobytes under a multi-gigabyte
         limit that is not a trade at all. On a server actually near its memory ceiling it would be,
         which is the line at which this whole file stops being appropriate. -->
    <memory_worker_period_ms>2000</memory_worker_period_ms>
</clickhouse>
